WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.714
Hello and welcome back to JD Science

2
00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.119
This GCSE Chemistry lesson is about rates of reaction

3
00:00:09.394 --> 00:00:12.533
By the end you should be able to do three things

4
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.679
Say what reaction rate means

5
00:00:16.659 --> 00:00:19.031
Calculate average rate from measurements

6
00:00:20.012 --> 00:00:22.810
And read graphs that show how the rate changes

7
00:00:24.094 --> 00:00:26.807
Some chemical reactions finish in a flash

8
00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:29.044
Others take years

9
00:00:30.322 --> 00:00:32.282
Fireworks are very fast

10
00:00:33.269 --> 00:00:35.556
They release energy in a bright burst

11
00:00:36.838 --> 00:00:38.500
Rusting of iron is slow

12
00:00:39.487 --> 00:00:43.109
Iron changes gradually as it reacts with oxygen and water

13
00:00:44.386 --> 00:00:47.809
The rate of a reaction tells us how quickly reactants are used

14
00:00:47.824 --> 00:00:48.079
up

15
00:00:48.363 --> 00:00:50.608
or how quickly products are formed

16
00:00:51.886 --> 00:00:54.457
Reactants are the substances we start with

17
00:00:55.444 --> 00:00:58.839
Products are the new substances made during the reaction

18
00:01:00.106 --> 00:01:04.339
Average rate equals the quantity of product formed divided by the time

19
00:01:04.353 --> 00:01:04.936
taken

20
00:01:05.922 --> 00:01:10.681
Or the quantity of reactant used up divided by the time taken

21
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.226
Quantity might be a mass in grams a volume in centimetres cubed

22
00:01:16.481 --> 00:01:17.930
or an amount in moles

23
00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:21.573
The units of rate follow the units you measured

24
00:01:22.553 --> 00:01:25.891
Grams divided by seconds gives grams per second

25
00:01:26.872 --> 00:01:31.531
Centimetres cubed divided by seconds gives centimetres cubed per second

26
00:01:32.794 --> 00:01:34.811
Let's use a familiar reaction

27
00:01:35.798 --> 00:01:40.414
Magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid to make magnesium chloride and

28
00:01:40.429 --> 00:01:41.181
hydrogen

29
00:01:42.162 --> 00:01:47.034
During the reaction the magnesium disappears and bubbles of hydrogen are given

30
00:01:47.048 --> 00:01:47.488
off

31
00:01:48.766 --> 00:01:51.891
We can collect the hydrogen and find the average rate

32
00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:58.034
Suppose we collect 180 centimetres cubed of hydrogen in 2 minutes

33
00:01:59.014 --> 00:02:03.034
We want the answer in centimetres cubed per second so convert the

34
00:02:03.048 --> 00:02:04.000
time first

35
00:02:05.266 --> 00:02:09.144
2 minutes times 60 equals 120 seconds

36
00:02:10.131 --> 00:02:12.361
Now divide the volume by the time

37
00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:18.369
180 divided by 120 equals 1.5

38
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:23.526
So the average rate is 1.5 centimetres cubed per second

39
00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.290
That is an average over the whole 2 minutes

40
00:02:28.277 --> 00:02:31.587
The actual rate may have been faster at the start and slower

41
00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:32.126
later

42
00:02:33.394 --> 00:02:36.718
We can also measure how quickly a reactant is used up

43
00:02:37.705 --> 00:02:42.563
In a separate experiment 3 grams of magnesium reacts completely in 4

44
00:02:42.577 --> 00:02:43.131
minutes

45
00:02:44.398 --> 00:02:46.600
Convert 4 minutes to seconds

46
00:02:47.587 --> 00:02:51.109
4 times 60 equals 240 seconds

47
00:02:52.089 --> 00:02:54.547
Then divide the mass used up by the time

48
00:02:55.527 --> 00:02:56.663
3 divided by

49
00:02:56.678 --> 00:03:01.010
240 equals 0.0125

50
00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:06.592
The average rate is 0.0125 grams per second

51
00:03:07.858 --> 00:03:10.628
Average rate hides how the reaction changes

52
00:03:11.615 --> 00:03:13.092
Graphs show that clearly

53
00:03:14.374 --> 00:03:17.897
First mass of magnesium remaining against time

54
00:03:18.884 --> 00:03:21.100
Time goes on the horizontal axis

55
00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.707
Mass remaining goes on the vertical axis

56
00:03:25.978 --> 00:03:29.472
If we start with 3 grams the line begins at 3

57
00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:34.195
As magnesium is used up the line slopes downwards

58
00:03:35.175 --> 00:03:38.527
It is steep at first because the reaction is fast

59
00:03:39.508 --> 00:03:41.113
Then it becomes less steep

60
00:03:42.093 --> 00:03:47.121
If all the magnesium reacts the line reaches zero and becomes horizontal

61
00:03:48.406 --> 00:03:51.744
Now look at volume of hydrogen produced against time

62
00:03:52.731 --> 00:03:56.808
The line starts at zero because no gas has been collected yet

63
00:03:57.788 --> 00:04:00.870
As hydrogen is produced the line rises

64
00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:05.231
It is steep at the beginning then less steep then it levels

65
00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:07.106
off when the reaction has finished

66
00:04:08.386 --> 00:04:12.179
For both graphs a steeper line means a faster reaction

67
00:04:13.166 --> 00:04:17.256
A horizontal line means the measured quantity is no longer changing

68
00:04:18.526 --> 00:04:21.765
A question might ask for moles per minute instead of grams per

69
00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:22.404
second

70
00:04:23.391 --> 00:04:25.053
Always check the units

71
00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:29.442
If it asks for a rate per second convert the time into

72
00:04:29.456 --> 00:04:31.203
seconds before you divide

73
00:04:32.482 --> 00:04:33.547
Remember this

74
00:04:34.534 --> 00:04:38.654
Average rate is the quantity of reactant used up or product formed

75
00:04:38.881 --> 00:04:40.671
divided by the time taken

76
00:04:41.651 --> 00:04:45.458
On a graph greater steepness means a faster reaction

77
00:04:46.726 --> 00:04:51.016
If this explanation has helped please subscribe to JD Science for more

78
00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:51.783
lessons

79
00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:57.869
You can find further learning resources at jdscience.co.uk

80
00:04:58.849 --> 00:05:00.085
Thank you for watching
